# [K50] (docs/dev/known_issues.md), found 2026-09-05 by lane k49fix while
# fixing K49 — by asking whether the OTHER engine's "try the next start"
# mechanism had the same hazard. It does, and unlike K49 it is reachable
# from an ORDINARY startpos=0.
#
# Under `-e utf8` the DFA's start-anywhere self-loop (src/ir/nfa.c:965,
# `nfa_wrap_unanchored`, a class of EVERY BYTE) lets an unanchored match
# START at any byte offset, so a zero-width match can be reported at an
# offset INSIDE a multi-byte character. `\B` over `61 CE B1` (ASCII `a`
# then alpha; character boundaries 0, 1 and 3) is reported at (2,2), and
# byte 2 is the second byte of alpha.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROVENANCE: THIS CELL IS NOT `pcrec-ARGUED`. IT HAS A REAL ORACLE.
#
# The manager's filing instruction asked for this to be marked
# `pcrec-ARGUED` like K49's cell was. That would be wrong here and the
# difference is worth keeping, so it is recorded rather than followed:
# K49's expectation was ARGUED because NO ENGINE PRODUCES THAT CELL AT
# ALL (a mid-loop retry past a caller-supplied startpos is pcrec's own
# next_pos-driven protocol, not something pcre2_match's startoffset is
# required to honour). This one is an ordinary unanchored search from
# offset 0, which every engine answers.
#
# THE REFERENCE ORACLE (libpcre2 10.46, ubuntubudu's system libpcre2 via
# ctypes; run by the manager 2026-09-05). Subject 61 CE B1, pattern \B,
# startoffset 0:
#
#     10.46 2025-08-27  options=0    rc=1  span=(2,2)
#     10.46 2025-08-27  PCRE2_UTF    rc=1  span=(3,3)
#
# DISCOVERY reading, kept because it is what found the bug and because the
# agreement across versions is itself worth recording (lane k49fix, same day,
# libpcre2 10.37 through docs/design/subroutines_measurements/probes/
# sr_oracle.py on the Mac dev box):
#
#     PCRE2_UTF                    startpos=0 -> (3,3)
#     PCRE2_UTF|MATCH_INVALID_UTF  startpos=0 -> (3,3)
#     options=0 (byte)             startpos=0 -> (2,2)
#
# The 10.37 reading is the LOCAL Miniconda build and is not this project's
# reference (memory `pcrec-cross-platform-verification`; U13/U14 are the
# recorded hazard of citing it as one) — the 10.46 line above is. They agree
# cell for cell, so the divergence is not a version artefact.
#
# Both UTF option words answer (3,3). pcrec under `-e utf8` answers
# (2,2) — which is exactly the `options=0` BYTE answer — so this is
# pcrec returning byte semantics from a UTF-8 build, and D26 (PCRE2 is
# the source of truth for semantics) settles it without anyone ruling.
# An oracle-backed cell is STRICTLY STRONGER evidence than an argued
# one; calling it ARGUED would understate the finding and would put a
# false provenance marker in the corpus.
#
# The design-side reasoning agrees independently, and is the reason the
# whole family is reachable: utf8_design.md Sec 2.6.1's inversion —
# "'No path' INVERTS for a negative assertion" — is what turns a
# mid-character start from a WASTED ATTEMPT into a WRONG ANSWER. `\B`
# is the same shape: it is true exactly where `\b` has no boundary, so
# a position no forward automaton can legitimately start at still
# ANSWERS. Sec 2.6.1.1's table is the measured form of the same point.
# A hand derivation gives (3,3) too: `\B` is false at 0 (start of
# subject beside the word character `a` IS a boundary), false at 1 (`a`
# then alpha crosses word/non-word), and true at 3 (alpha precedes, end
# of subject follows, neither is a word character).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE VM HALF OF THIS WAS K49 AND IS FIXED; this is the DFA half, and the
# two engines implement "try the next start" by different mechanisms, so
# K49's fix does not reach it. Before K49 both engines answered (2,2) —
# wrong in agreement, which is why no cross-engine check caught either.
#
# Kept HERE per this directory's convention: the cell stays, it stays
# loud, and the ratchet FIRES the moment the DFA half is fixed.

# pcre2-only
pattern \B
encoding utf8
features assertions
m "a\xce\xb1" 3 3
